// Cairo's own surface, declared verbatim — **everything in this package that is C lives here**.
//
// **Nothing here is meant to be called by a program.** Every name and every convention is C's: a
// matrix is six doubles behind a pointer, a string is a NUL-terminated `*u8`, an enumeration is an
// `int`. The modules beside this one turn those into sysl and are what an application imports.
//
// The module is named `c` because that is what it is: every `@link`, every `@include`, every
// `c const`, every opaque handle and every `extern` in this package is in this one file, and a call
// site next door reads `c.surface_destroy(h)` — which says where it goes without a comment. A
// package that carried its own `.c` files would put them in this directory too.
//
// The split keeps two jobs apart. This one has to be **faithful** — a signature that disagrees with
// the C header links perfectly and corrupts the call at run time, and nothing in this arrangement
// checks one against the other, so each declaration below was read out of `cairo.h` rather than
// remembered. The other one has to be **pleasant**, which is a different question and would
// otherwise be answered in the same breath.
//
// **Not one public function in cairo takes or returns a struct by value**, which was checked by
// walking every `cairo_public` declaration in the header rather than by reading a few of them: the
// only non-scalar parameters in the whole library are enums, which are `int`s, and function
// pointers. So there is no shim in this package at all.
//
// The structs cairo does have — a matrix, the two kinds of extents, a glyph — are always written
// through a pointer into storage the *caller* owns, which is the house pattern and needs nothing
// from the language beyond taking a struct's address.

module sh.sysl.cairo.c
@link("cairo")


// ## The values
//
// **No number is written here.** Each is what the C compiler computes for cairo's own name, for the
// target being built — so a transposed digit is impossible rather than merely unlikely. `15 §7`
// calls a transcribed constant "correct on one machine" with "nothing checking it", and a
// `c const` block is what it asks for instead.
//
// They sit in this module rather than beside the enums that use them for the same reason the
// `extern`s do: this is the layer that speaks C, and a `CAIRO_STATUS_SUCCESS` is as much a C name
// as a `cairo_create` is. The module next door turns them into a `Status`.
//
// Three headers, because cairo puts the vector backends' values in their own.

@include("cairo.h")
@include("cairo-pdf.h")
@include("cairo-svg.h")

c const
    STATUS_SUCCESS: int = "CAIRO_STATUS_SUCCESS"
    STATUS_NO_MEMORY: int = "CAIRO_STATUS_NO_MEMORY"
    STATUS_INVALID_RESTORE: int = "CAIRO_STATUS_INVALID_RESTORE"
    STATUS_INVALID_POP_GROUP: int = "CAIRO_STATUS_INVALID_POP_GROUP"
